  "query_preview": "..Water vapor imagery shows a shortwave exiting\nArizona with a larger closed low entering the northern Great\nBasin. The passing shortwave has lead to breezy winds this\nafternoon. Current gusts range from 20 to 30 mph and will\ncontinue through the afternoon and early evening. Thursday the\nclosed low stays to the north and moves into the Dakotas with a\nshortwave moving around its southern periphery near the AZ/UT\nborder and the Four Corners region. This will lead to another\nround of breezy winds, slightly stronger than today, from \ncentral Pima County eastward. Winds will be below Red Flag \ncriteria both today and tomorrow, but will be strong enough to \nlead to elevated fire weather conditions both days. Temperatures\nalso cool a few degrees to around normal Thursday. \n\nFriday will see mostly zonal flow aloft before the next trough\nmoves through the region this weekend. Winds remain slightly\nelevated with speeds 10-15 mph in the afternoon. This weekend a\nsmall closed low moves onshore into southern California then\nopens up and travels through Arizona. The jet associated with\nthe low will pass right over southeast Arizona, mixing down\nmomentum which will lead to breezy to windy conditions both\nSaturday and Sunday. Although Sunday it looks like the stronger\nwinds will be confined to areas east of Tucson. Expect sustained\nspeeds of 15-25 mph and gusts 30-40 mph both days with Sunday \nwinds likely a little stronger than Saturday. Some areas of \ncritical fire weather conditions may be possible Saturday with \nRH values 10-15 percent in lower elevation valleys. However \nsurface moisture does begin to creep into western Pima County \nSaturday afternoon. Depending on how far east this moisture \nmakes it, RH values could be impacted which would reduce the \nthreat of Red Flag conditions. Sunday the moisture makes it into\nthe eastern valleys and temperatures cool 4-6 degrees below \nnormal, which will keep RH values around 15-25 percent for most\nlocations. The NBM keeps precip chances north of our area \nSunday but ensembles, mainly the EPS, do show a slight chance \nfor showers in Pinal County and northern Graham/Greenlee \nCounties.\n\nEarly next week it looks like southeast Arizona will get a brief\nbreak from the breezy winds before ensembles bring another\nPacific low through the region by the middle of the week. Higher\nconfidence that this low will bring more breezy winds Wednesday,\nand lower confidence that it will bring some precipitation. \nRight now NBM PoPs are 15-30 percent east of Tucson Wednesday. \nEnsemble probabilities are similar.",
